STORY = """
<h2>Pipeline Map</h2>
<p>Most people picture power as something won: an election, a revolution, a coup. The biographies of the people who hold it often show an earlier step. Before they won anything, someone picked them. A scholarship committee, a foundation, an embassy, a party school, a seminary or a regiment saw a promising young person, paid for a year or a degree somewhere far from home, and introduced them to people they would deal with for the rest of their lives.</p>
<p>This map follows those pipelines. Each circle is a school or programme that trains young people who go on to rule. Each line runs from that school to a country where one of its former students became a head of state or government, a central bank governor or a minister. The thicker the line, the more of them.</p>
<h3>Where it started for this map</h3>
<p>The Welcome to Your Galaxy page on suppression notes that a few Western universities, Harvard, Chicago and the LSE among them, and think tanks such as the Council on Foreign Relations and Chatham House, train the future leaders of the Global South in theories that justify the existing order, such as the idea that free trade is always good. The best-documented example is Chile. In 1956 the US government paid the University of Chicago to train Chilean economics students. When General Pinochet seized power in 1973, those students, the Chicago Boys, took over the economy and opened it to foreign capital. The Ford Foundation did the same thing in Indonesia through Berkeley. The infiltration map adds the World Economic Forum's Young Global Leaders, whose founder said in 2017: "we penetrate the cabinets".</p>
<h3>The kinds of pipeline</h3>
<p>Some pipelines teach a doctrine, like Chicago's economics or a party school's Marxism. Some are run by governments to win friends abroad, like the US State Department's visitor programme, which only embassy staff can nominate for, or Britain's Chevening scholarships, which the British government calls an investment in its long-term influence. Some train army officers, and a number of those officers later took power in coups. Some belong to religious movements. Some are simply the schools a country's rulers have always attended.</p>
<p>The patterns box on the map groups these into ten ways the pipelines work. Under them are a few possible patterns: things that are probably happening but have not been proved.</p>
<h3>What this map does not show</h3>
<p>A line on this map means someone studied or trained at a place and later held office. It does not prove that the school told them what to do. Many of these programmes pick people who were already likely to succeed and then take credit for them. But the people who run the programmes say plainly that influence is the point, and when the same few places keep producing the people who run the world, it is fair to ask who chose them, who paid, and what they learned there.</p>
"""

METHOD = """
<h2>Method and sources</h2>
<h3>The schools and programmes</h3>
<p>The map holds __NS__ documented schools, academies, fellowships, networks and employers that train young or mid-career people who later hold power. They were chosen because a published source documents their role in producing leaders; each school's box gives its sources. They are grouped into nine kinds. A school is placed at its campus or headquarters; programmes run by a ministry are placed at that ministry.</p>
<h3>Every other school in the record</h3>
<p>Behind the documented schools sit __NR__ more: every other school, university, academy or seminary that Wikidata records as attended by someone who later held one of the offices below. These are the small grey dots. No one has written about most of them as a pipeline; they are shown so that nothing in the record is left out and you can see which places keep turning up. Colleges and faculties are folded into the university they belong to. A record school is placed at the location Wikidata gives it, or its parent's location if it has none; a few hundred have no location at all and appear only in the lists and search.</p>
<h3>The office-holders</h3>
<p>The office-holders come from Wikidata, the structured database behind Wikipedia, read on __DATE__. A person is linked to a school when Wikidata records them as educated at it (or at one of its colleges or faculties), as a recipient of its scholarship, as a member of it, or, for employers and think tanks only, as a former employee. Lists of members published on Wikipedia were also read for programmes Wikidata does not cover well: the International Visitor Leadership Program, Chevening, Fulbright, Rhodes, Marshall, the Young Global Leaders, the French-American Foundation, Atlantik-Brücke, Eisenhower Fellowships, YSEALI, the Mont Pelerin Society, the Chicago Boys, the School of the Americas, the Command and General Staff College and Old Etonians. Each person links to their Wikidata record.</p>
<p>For a university, the office-holders are all of its recorded alumni, not only those of the department its box describes: the University of Chicago line includes Chicago lawyers as well as the Chicago Boys.</p>
<p>An office counts when Wikidata records it as a national post of head of state, head of government, minister or central bank governor. Offices of historical states are assigned to today's successor state (the Soviet Union to Russia, West Germany to Germany, Yugoslavia to Serbia). Regional, state and city offices are left out.</p>
<p>In total: __NP__ people holding __NO__ national offices, linked to __NL__ school-person pairs, in __NC__ countries.</p>
<h3>How much of the world this covers</h3>
<p>Wikidata lists about __NALL__ people who have held one of these national offices. Roughly half of them have a school recorded. The map shows every one who does. The other half are not missing by choice; their education has simply not been entered. Each country's box gives its own figure: how many office-holders Wikidata lists and how many have a school on record.</p>
<p>What cannot be counted is the programmes Wikidata does not track at all: short visitor programmes, party schools, private fellowships and nomination schemes whose alumni lists are not published. The documented schools cover the best-known of these, but there are certainly more.</p>
<h3>Behind the scenes</h3>
<p>Each country's box opens with a short account of how its rulers were trained, written from the office-holders in the record and checked against Wikipedia and the sources listed under it. There are __NACC__ of these. They describe who studied where and what followed; they do not claim that any school directed what its students later did.</p>
<h3>Corrections to the record</h3>
<p>Wikidata is edited by volunteers and contains errors. A few were fixed for this map: city mayors that Wikidata files as heads of government were removed; a fictional character recorded as a US president was removed; two office names damaged by vandalism were restored; about sixty names that came back in a non-Latin script were replaced with their English labels. Other oddities are left as recorded, such as a school placed in the wrong country. If you see one, it is in Wikidata and can be corrected there.</p>
<h3>Limits</h3>
<ul>
<li>Wikidata is uneven. It records education and offices well for Europe, North America and parts of Asia, and less well elsewhere. A country with few lines may be under-recorded, not untouched.</li>
<li>Ministers are recorded less completely than heads of state or government, so the heads-only filter is the fairer comparison between countries.</li>
<li>Monarchs count as heads of state, so the British king appears in each Commonwealth realm where he is head of state.</li>
<li>A line shows that a person attended a place and later held office. It does not show that the place caused or directed what they did.</li>
<li>Short programmes such as the International Visitor Leadership Program last weeks, not years. They are on the map because the programmes themselves count their alumni in power.</li>
<li>Nothing has been left out by choice: every link Wikidata and the published lists hold for these schools and these offices is shown, and the filters let you narrow it.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Figures quoted in the boxes</h3>
<p>Counts such as 'more than 350 heads of state' for the International Visitor Leadership Program or '22 heads of state or government' for Chevening are the programmes' own figures, quoted from their publications. The map's own counts can differ because Wikidata and published lists are incomplete.</p>
<h3>Main sources</h3>
<ul>
<li>Wikidata, <a href="https://query.wikidata.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">query service</a> (education, awards, memberships, employers, positions held)</li>
<li>Wikipedia lists and articles named in each school's box</li>
<li>Savage and Caverley, 'When human capital threatens the Capitol', Journal of Peace Research, 2017</li>
<li>Chevening Impact Report 2026; Institute of International Education, IVLP; US State Department, Fulbright overview</li>
<li>Africa Center for Strategic Studies and South China Morning Post on the Nyerere Leadership School</li>
<li>US Treasury press release of 8 December 2020 on Al-Mustafa International University</li>
<li>Latin America Working Group on the declassified School of the Americas manuals</li>
</ul>
<p>Basemaps from the Culprits map: Esri imagery, OpenFreeMap and OpenStreetMap, Mapterhorn and AWS terrain, CARTO labels.</p>
"""
